\paragraph{Descriptive execution analyses.}
The primary evaluator favors longer outputs overall, and the length- and compute-matched analyses are descriptive because both variables result from execution.
Process traces likewise record state transitions and agent actions; they characterize how \condAgenticCogWriter\ behaves on these tasks but do not measure human cognition or validate cognitive process theory as a model of language-agent internals.

These matching-band results are descriptive sensitivity checks; ties are excluded from the win-rate denominator, and generation runs are reported separately because they reuse the same prompts.
The \condSingleWriter\ length-band tests in Section~\ref{sec:results-mechanism} instead combine the three runs, so their sign tests take the prompt as the unit: a prompt counts as a win or a loss when its outcomes in the band, averaged across runs, favor one system.
